# Voice

Copy this file to `voice.md` and replace every line with your own words.

This is the only file in `candidate_kb/` that is NOT about what you achieved.
`resume_bullets.md` and `projects.md` already hold the achievements, and a cover
letter written from those alone reads as a restatement of your CV — metric,
metric, metric — which is exactly what an AI-written letter reads like.

What a human letter adds is here: why this kind of work, what a number actually
meant day to day, which problems you enjoy. The generator cannot invent any of
that without fabricating, and the verifier will flag it if it tries. Writing it
down is what makes personal texture *grounded*.

Rules of thumb:
- Write in the first person, in your normal speaking voice. Fragments are fine.
- Be specific and slightly unflattering. "I get impatient with systems nobody
  can explain" is usable; "I am a passionate team player" is not.
- Never invent. If a claim is not true of you, the verifier catching it is the
  system working.
- Unlike the other KB files, this one is NOT indexed into Qdrant — it is
  appended to every generation, so no rebuild is needed after editing it.

---

## Why this kind of work

I keep coming back to backend and data plumbing. I like taking something slow,
manual, or hard to trust and making it boring to run.

The part I enjoy is not the framework — it is the moment a process nobody wanted
to touch becomes something you can hand to someone else.

## What the numbers actually meant

When I halved the database at Musterfirma, the storage bill was not the point.
It meant the nightly job stopped paging people at 3am.

The 40 hours a month of reporting I automated were 40 hours the team had been
spending on work they resented.

## Problems I like

Messy inputs. Sources that disagree with each other, data that changes shape
without warning, services that answer slowly instead of failing outright.

Deciding what a user should see when the system is not sure. I would rather show
an honest "I do not know" than a confident wrong answer.

## What I want next

I want to work closer to the data infrastructure rather than only consuming it.
I have operated these systems in production; I want to design them.

I would like to be somewhere with people who are better at this than I am. Most
of what I know I learned from code review, not documentation.

## How I work with people

I write things down. If a decision only exists in someone's head, it will be
re-litigated in three months.

I would rather ship something small that works and iterate than plan for a year.
